WISP LOCAL BROWSER \u2014 THIRD-PARTY NOTICES

This file includes the full license notices for the installed runtime
dependency set (including transitive dependencies), native libraries in
libcurl.js, the Go modules in the Chrome-fingerprint transport, and supplementary toolchain notices. Some installed dependencies
may be removed by the bundler. Their inclusion here does not change their
license or assign a license to the new application code. No application
license or author attribution has been invented.

libcurl.js is used under LGPL-3.0-or-later; the complete LGPL version 3 and
GPL version 3 texts are included below. This provenance package documents
what was collected; it does not claim legal compliance or a bit-for-bit
reproducible upstream build. Read the source gaps and rebuild instructions.


==============================================================================
SOURCE PROVENANCE
==============================================================================
# Collected source and license provenance

This package contains source archives and full notices, rather than relying only
on links. It is an engineering provenance record, not a legal-compliance opinion.
The new application has no license selected yet. No author has been invented.
Scramjet was inspected as a reference; no Scramjet source was copied into this
application, so its license is not presented as an application dependency.

## Exact published JavaScript dependencies

\`npm-source-archives.json\` identifies all 19 installed runtime package versions,
including transitive dependencies and both versions of \`entities\`. Each listed
archive is included in \`sources/\`; its integrity was checked against
\`package-lock.json\`. \`runtime-dependencies.json\` records the installed versions
and the full notices copied into \`npm/\`. MIT, BSD, Apache, CC0 and LGPL notices
are reproduced, without assuming that every JavaScript dependency is MIT.
Package archives contain the release's published JavaScript, maps and any
included original sources. Registry-reported \`gitHead\` and repository metadata
are recorded where available; the archives are not claimed to include every
upstream repository's unpublished build inputs or development tests.

The Public Suffix List data used by tldts has its own MPL-2.0 license. Its full
license and source archive are included separately. The exact data revision,
a179a48c465e818cfd8d626691cb317985da87fb, is the \`publicsuffix\` gitlink in the
tldts 7.4.16 registry-reported source commit
b9519e00ba47ad557cc9fc0416ea0cf5f89a4a12. See \`data-source-archives.json\`.

## libcurl.js 0.7.4

Exact published artifact:
https://registry.npmjs.org/libcurl.js/-/libcurl.js-0.7.4.tgz

Registry SHA-1: 13c71e12ee4724ff4c9264f16f02cf02830ea0eb

Registry/lockfile integrity:
sha512-UpvVirvATP7fD0t4rnsxVRuUpPVIo2QvWj4+5JrMsd1KSEvYkON36+COOPAl88hPlGJddk+DfZRvyF7KG7YcSA==

The registry \`gitHead\` is a641cdd857356db6b8ed46c5341cc8f85c4190ba.
It matches the read-only reference checkout at
\`work/references/wisp-libcurl-js\` used during development. A complete tracked
source snapshot from that commit is included as
\`sources/libcurl.js-a641cdd857356db6b8ed46c5341cc8f85c4190ba.tar.gz\`, including
the client JavaScript, C wrappers, patches, build scripts and license.

Upstream source:
https://github.com/ading2210/libcurl.js/tree/a641cdd857356db6b8ed46c5341cc8f85c4190ba

The client/Wisp submodule is separately included at its exact pinned commit:
https://github.com/MercuryWorkshop/wisp-client-js/tree/455b9186bfbd0915ed7082c9d356af2157ab014f

The upstream npm library files have not been edited. Application code wraps
selected request cancellation and socket lifecycle behavior at runtime; those
changes are visible in this application's \`src/transport.js\` and related files.
The library's LGPL-3.0-or-later notice is preserved. Complete LGPL version 3 and
GPL version 3 texts are included as \`LGPL-3.0.txt\` and \`GPL-3.0.txt\`.

## Native sources inside the WebAssembly

The exact upstream build-script refs are curl \`8.17.0-patched\`, mbedTLS
\`mbedtls-3.6.5\`, cJSON \`v1.7.19\`, zlib \`v1.3.1\`, Brotli \`v1.2.0\`, and nghttp2
\`v1.68.0\`. Archives and SHA-256 digests are recorded in
\`native-source-archives.json\` and included in \`sources/\`. Full native library
license texts are in \`upstream/\`. The mbedTLS framework submodule is included
separately at 457996474728cb8e968ed21953b72f74d2f536b2, its exact 3.6.5 gitlink.

The published WASM was initialized locally with all network requests blocked.
Its runtime version report is retained in \`libcurl-runtime-versions.json\`:
libcurl.js 0.7.4, Wisp client 1.1.1, curl 8.17.0-DEV, mbedTLS 3.6.5,
Brotli 1.2.0 and nghttp2 1.68.0. Its embedded 148-certificate trust store was
recovered through the library's own \`get_cacert()\` API and included as
\`sources/libcurl.js-0.7.4-cacert.pem\`; this avoids substituting today's CA bundle.

## Known source/reproduction gaps

- The curl build script uses a mutable branch, not an immutable commit. The
  included curl source is its resolved commit
  a15072369578a37ce314ea4d8b06f30c2762ebcc at collection time. The npm publication
  does not prove that this is the precise checkout used for its binary.
- Upstream installs the distro's Emscripten and other build tools without pinning
  the release environment. Its README documents Emscripten 3.1.6 and 3.1.72 as
  tested versions. The precise compiler, bundled libc/compiler-rt objects and
  system build-tool versions used for the npm WASM are not identified. The
  Emscripten 3.1.6 license file is included as supplementary runtime/toolchain
  notices, not as proof of the actual compiler version. A matching complete
  toolchain source snapshot was not collected.
- Upstream's server/Wisp Python submodule is not included: this application uses
  the client/WASM and does not distribute that server. The archive retains its
  gitlink metadata only; it is not needed to rebuild the client.
- These archives and scripts were collected and checked, but the native library
  was not rebuilt during this task. No byte-identical native rebuild is claimed.

The inert notices panel in \`index.html\` includes license texts, provenance and
rebuild directions. The adjacent \`licenses/\` directory contains the actual
source archives; they are not embedded into the HTML. Preserve this source
package with distributions or arrange a source delivery mechanism appropriate
to the applicable licenses before distributing the HTML by itself.


==============================================================================
REBUILDING AND REPLACING LIBRARIES
==============================================================================
# Rebuilding and replacing bundled libraries

The HTML is generated, not a required signed or locked artifact. The application
source and build scripts are included; no technical restriction is added to
inspecting or replacing the bundled libraries. This document grants no new
license to the application code. Its license remains for the user to decide.

## Rebuild the current application

From the application directory, with Node.js and npm installed:

    npm ci --ignore-scripts
    npm run build

The build reads the lockfile, verifies the included source-archive digests,
collects full notices from every installed runtime dependency, and writes
\`index.html\`, \`THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt\` and the installed dependency manifest.
The resulting HTML embeds its JavaScript, WASM and readable inert notices;
it does not fetch a runtime library from a CDN. Developer-only tools are not
classified as runtime dependencies merely because they are installed.

## Rebuild or modify libcurl.js

The upstream native build is documented for Linux. Use a Linux environment with
Emscripten, git, make, cmake, autoconf, automake, libtool, pkg-config, wget, xxd,
jq and Python 3. Read the included upstream README and build scripts before
running them. No native compiler or OS toolchain is supplied here.

Extract \`sources/libcurl.js-a641cdd857356db6b8ed46c5341cc8f85c4190ba.tar.gz\`.
Extract \`sources/wisp-client-js-455b9186bfbd0915ed7082c9d356af2157ab014f.tar.gz\`
into that source tree's \`client/wisp_client/\`, dropping the archive's first
path component. This restores the precise client submodule omitted by git
archive. Make any desired library modifications in that extracted tree.

The provided upstream scripts fetch their native dependencies using the refs
listed in PROVENANCE.md. Copies of those native sources are also supplied here.
For an offline native build, adjust the clone/extraction steps in each
\`client/tools/*.sh\` to unpack the corresponding local archive; restore the
mbedTLS framework archive into mbedTLS's \`framework/\` directory. The native
build scripts otherwise retain upstream behavior, including compiler flags.

To use the recovered release trust store, first run the upstream dependency
build, then generate its expected header explicitly (the upstream certificate
script skips generation if its PEM file already exists):

    cd /absolute/path/to/extracted-libcurl-source/client
    ./tools/all_deps.sh
    cp /absolute/path/to/application/licenses/sources/libcurl.js-0.7.4-cacert.pem build/cacert.pem
    python3 tools/gen_cert.py build/cacert.pem > build/curl-wasm/include/cacert.h
    ./build.sh all
    cp package.json ../README.md ../LICENSE ../CHANGELOG.md out/

\`out/\` now contains a replacement npm package, including the regular and
single-file WASM builds. \`./publish.sh\` is not needed and would publish upstream;
do not run it for a local replacement. This task did not execute the native
build, and the upstream environment gaps prevent a byte-identical guarantee.

## Override the npm library and relink the HTML

From this application's directory:

    npm install --save-exact --ignore-scripts --install-links /absolute/path/to/extracted-libcurl-source/client/out
    npm run build

This replaces the direct \`libcurl.js\` dependency with your local package and
updates the lockfile; esbuild then embeds that replacement in \`index.html\`.
If an npm overrides entry is desired, reference the direct dependency rather
than conflicting with it:

    "dependencies": { "libcurl.js": "file:/absolute/path/to/extracted-libcurl-source/client/out" },
    "overrides": { "libcurl.js": "$libcurl.js" }

The recorded original npm archive remains a reference, not the source of your
modified build. Before redistributing a replacement, retain your changed
library sources/build scripts, describe modifications, include their notices,
and update the source manifests and provenance. Merely retaining the old
upstream links does not provide the source of your modified library.

## Rebuild or modify the Chrome-fingerprint transport

The default transport is a Go program compiled to WebAssembly. Its source is in
\`native/chrome-transport\` and its module versions are pinned by \`go.sum\`. With
Go installed:

    node scripts/build-transport.mjs
    npm run build

The first command writes \`vendor/chrome-transport.wasm.gz\`, \`vendor/wasm_exec.js\`
and the manifests under \`vendor/\`; the second embeds them in \`index.html\`.
Without Go, \`npm run build\` reuses the committed \`vendor/\` files. The module
list, Go version and SHA-256 of every output are recorded in
\`vendor/chrome-transport.json\`, and the full license text of each compiled Go
module in \`vendor/chrome-transport-notices.json\`. libcurl.js remains available
as an opt-in fallback transport.

## Rebuild or modify the WebRTC engine

The WebRTC stack is a second Go program compiled to WebAssembly, built on
Pion (\`github.com/pion/webrtc/v4\`, MIT). Its source is in
\`native/webrtc-engine\`, with module versions pinned by \`go.sum\`. With Go
installed:

    node scripts/build-webrtc.mjs
    npm run build

Pion's \`js/wasm\` build wraps the browser's own \`RTCPeerConnection\`, which
would open UDP sockets outside Wisp. The build script therefore compiles a
patched temporary copy of Pion with those browser wrapper files removed and
the pure-Go implementation selected (53 files un-tagged, 15 wrapper files
dropped; the patched copy is referenced through a generated \`-modfile\` and is
never written into the module cache). The command writes
\`vendor/webrtc-engine.wasm.gz\` and \`vendor/webrtc-engine.json\` (module list, Go
version, SHA-256 of every output), and the full license text of each compiled
module goes to \`vendor/webrtc-engine-notices.json\`. Without Go, \`npm run
build\` reuses the committed \`vendor/\` files. The offline engine tests drive a
native Pion peer (\`native/webrtc-engine/cmd/testpeer\`) and need Go as well.


